                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_string_theory"} In string theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_topological_phases_of_matter"} In topological phases of matter
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that is induced from certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometric
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomological
            data, to be thought of as encoding the background geometry on which quantum objects of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            propagate.
          %p
            The operation of
            %strong T-duality
            is a map that interchanges pairs of such geometric data for 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
          %p
            More specifically the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometric
            data consisting of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a smooth
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                equipped with the structure of a
                = succeed "-torus" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ×
                        %msup
                          %mi T
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \simeq Y \times T^k
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %li
              %p
                and equipped with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                – modelling the field of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %li
              %p
                and a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
                with connection
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %li
              %p
                possibly a cocycle in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
                modelling the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
          %p
            admits a certain operation that, roughly, inverts the Riemannian circumference of the torus fibers and mixes the metric with the gerbe data, such that the induced 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            QFTs for these backgrounds are equivalent. This is the operation called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong T-duality
          %p
            This was noticed originally in the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theories
            in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            turn out to be equivalent as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theories
            for T-dual backgrounds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g,G)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X',g',G')
            (at least to the approximate degree to which these are realized as full CFTs in the first place).
          %p
            Further generalisations let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a nontrivial torus bundle, but the T-dual is then generically a bundle of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+torus"} non-commutative tori
          %h2#PathIntegral Path integral heuristics deriving T-duality
          %p
            We indicate how one can see T-duality from formal manipulations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            carrying the metric induced from the standard flat metric on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            and where there are no other nontrivial background fields. In fact, for the purpose of the following computation we can entirely ignore the base of this bundle and consider target space to be nothing but a circle. Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for this is on the worldsheet just the theory of a single free field with values in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
          %p
            This means that the only geometric datum determining the background geometry is the circumference
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \pi R
            of the fiber of the circle bundle. The statement of T-duality in this situation is that the 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            on this background yields the same 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            as that for this kind of background with circumference of the circle being
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \pi 1/R
          %h3#a_first_rough_look A first rough look
          %p
            A quick way to get an indication for this is to consider the center-of-mass energy of the string in such a circle-bundle background. In the simplified setup we mentioned before, a string on a circle of radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            has quantized momentum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℤ
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = \frac{\ell \in \mathbb{Z}}{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            times and has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            quanta of kinetic momentum for propagation around the circle, its energy is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi R
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mi m
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_0 = \sqrt{p^2 + M^2} = \sqrt{(\ell/R)^2 + (R m)^2 }
                  \\,,
          %p This energy is clearly invariant under exchanging
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (R, (\ell, m)) \mapsto (\frac{1}{R}, (m,\ell))
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is of course far from being a proof that the corresponding two
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
          %p In slightly more detail, but still at a very rough level, if we denote by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X : \Sigma \to S^1_R
          %p
            the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}^2
            with values in target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_R
            then T-duality with respect to this circle may be thought of as exchanging worldsheet momentum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi t
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_t X
            with worldsheet winding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi σ
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_\sigma X
          %p
            This then also means that for the open string it exchanges von Neumann boundary conditions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi σ
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_\sigma X|_{\sigma = 0} = 0
            with Dirichlet boundary conditions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_t X|_{\sigma = 0} = 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            in target space: the locus where the endpoints of the string are fixed. This is a first indication that the T-duality operation on geometric background also involves the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
          %h3#the_path_integral The path integral
          %p
            We follow
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kentaro+Hori"} Kentaro Hori
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            discussion of T-duality. Here the strategy is to consider a path integral over a certain space of auxiliary fields and show or argue that by “algebraically integrating out” some of these in two different ways, the path integral is equivalent to that over two different
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %p
            Let the boundary components of the worldsheet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be labeled by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma_{(1)}
          %p We consider the following fields on the worldsheet:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo :
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi R
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X : \Sigma \to \mathbb{R}/(2\pi/R)\mathbb{Z} = S^1_{1/R}
                – a circle-valued function; this is the standard
                = succeed "-model" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi R
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{i} : \partial \Sigma_{(i)} \to S^1_R
                – the boundary values of this field;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in \Omega^1(\Sigma, \mathbb{R})
                – a 1-form; this is the auxiliary field that will not contribute to the dynamics but serves to make the T-duality manifest.
          %p
            Consider then the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on this collection of fields given by the assignment
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi b
                    %mo −
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo )
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S'_E(\tilde X,b) = \frac{1}{2 \pi}
                  \\int_\Sigma
                  \\left(
                  \\frac{1}{2} b \wedge \star b  - b \wedge d \tilde X
                  \\right)
                  \-
                  \\frac{i}{2 \pi} \sum_{i = 1}^s
                  \\int_{\partial \Sigma_{(i)}}
                  (\tilde X -a_i) d X_i
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_i)
            are a collection of real numbers.
          %p
            We now want to formally perform the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            over the fields in two different orders, which should give the same quantum field theories but in terms of different effective action functionals.
          %p
            If we do first the path integral over the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            then by the general formal rule of “algebraically integrating out a non-dynamical field” which says that we can evaluate this path integral that formally looks like a Gaussian integral by the usual formulas for Gaussian integrals, we obtain the action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi π
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde S_E =
                  \\frac{1}{4 \pi}
                  \\int_\Sigma d \tilde X \wedge \star d \tilde X
                  +
                  \\frac{i}{2\pi}
                  \\int_{\partial \Sigma_{(i)}}
                  (\tilde X - a_i)
                  d X_i
          %p
            then doing the integral over the boundary values
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
            yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde X|_{\partial \Sigma_i} = a_i
          %p
            This is the action functional for a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi R
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_{1/R}
            with a D-brane at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X = a_i
          %p
            Now we evaluate the original path integral in a different way, this way first integrating over components of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
            in terms of its de Rham differential
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                    %mi R
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d \tilde X = d f + \frac{2\pi}{R}\sum_A \eta_A \omega_A
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_A
            are integers and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mn 2
                      %mi g
                      %mo +
                      %mi s
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\omega_A\} \subset
                  H^1(\Sigma, \mathbb{Z})
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathbb{Z}^{\oplus 2g + s - 1}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then formally performing the path integral over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            yields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d b = 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Σ
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b|_{\partial \Sigma} = d X_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = d X
            for some other field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X : \Sigma \to S^1_R
          %p So we get the action
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi π
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi d
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi d
                  %mi X
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mi d
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_E = \frac{1}{4\pi} \int_\Sigma d X \wedge \star d X
                  +
                  \\frac{i}{2 \pi}
                  \\sum_{i = 1}^s
                  \\int_{\partial \Sigma_i}
                  a_i d X
          %p
            in terms of the field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1_R
            that carries on its worldvolume a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            given by a constant connection 1-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
          %h2#topological_tduality Topological T-duality
          %p
            It turns out to be possible and useful to discuss just the
            %em topological
            aspects of T-duality, meaning all the aspects that depend on the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            and of its 3-form curvature, but not on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            and not on the precise connection on the gerbe (there may be several inequivalent ones for a given curvature)!
          %p
            This sub-phenomenon is discussed in more detail at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
          %h2#geometric_tduality Geometric T-duality
          %h3#InDiffCohomology In terms of generalized differential cohomology
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"}> Gauge field
            s are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            is given by degree-3
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
          %p
            Induced by the morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{c}(n)
            in the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequence"} fiber sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi PU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi c
                        %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1) \to \mathbf{B} U(n) \to \mathbf{B} PU(n)
                  \\stackrel{\mathbf{c}_n}{\to} \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)
          %p
            is induced a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            which makes the Kalb-Ramond field act as a twist for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
          %p
            In these terms, the setup of T-duality is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat P \to X
            that induces an integral transform
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi K
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi K
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi τ
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_{diff}^{\bullet + \tau}(P)
                  \\to
                  K_{diff}^{\bullet + \tau -1}(\hat P)
          %p of twisted differential K-theory classes.
          %p
            This is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            – the action of the T-duality isomorphism on the Kalb-Ramond field and the RR-field.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#KahleValentino"}> KahleValentino
            ).
          %h3#TdualityIngcg In generalized complex geometry
          %p
            Another approach to the study of T-duality takes a somewhat complementary point of view and ignores the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} integral cohomology
            class in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^3(X,\mathbb{Z})
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            but does consider the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
          %p
            In this context T-duality is described as an isomorphism of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+Courant+algebroid"} standard Courant algebroid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+complex+geometry"} generalized complex geometry
          %h2#examples_of_tdual_pairs Examples of T-dual pairs
          %h3#in_mirror_symmetry In Mirror symmetry
          %p
            One special case of T-duality is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Strominger"}> Andrew Strominger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shing-Tung+Yau"} Shing-Tung Yau
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Zaslow"} Eric Zaslow
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mirror Symmetry is T-Duality
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9606040"} hep-th/9606040
          %h3#in_langlands_duality In Langlands duality
          %p
            In some cases the passage to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
            can be understood as T-duality. (
            %a{:href => "#DaenzerErp"}> Daenzer-vanErp
            )
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+physics"}> duality in physics
            ,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong T-duality
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+T-duality"} spherical T-duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson-Lie+T-duality"} Poisson-Lie T-duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+T-duality"} differential T-duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality+2-group"} T-duality 2-group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-fold"}> T-fold
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"} double field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"}> U-duality
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          %p
            The geometry of the fiber product of two torus fiber bundles with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundle"} circle 2-bundle
            on it is sometimes referred to as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bn-geometry"} Bn-geometry
          %h2#references References
          %h3#in_string_theory In string theory
          %p The observation of T-duality is attributed to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Buscher"}> Thomas Buscher
                ,
                %em
                  A symmetry of the string background field equations, Phys. Lett. B 194 (1987) 59 (
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(87)90769-6"}> doi:10.1016/0370-2693(87)90769-6
                  )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Buscher"}> Thomas Buscher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Path integral derivation of quantum duality in nonlinear sigma models
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(88)90602-8"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(88)90602-8
          %p
            Precursors include (according to
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Schwarz96"} Schwarz 96, first paragraph
          %ul
            %li
              Keiji Kikkawa, Masami Yamasaki,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Casimir effects in superstring theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(84)90423-4"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(84)90423-4
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amit+Giveon"}> Amit Giveon
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Massimo+Porrati"} Massimo Porrati
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eliezer+Rabinovici"} Eliezer Rabinovici
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Target space duality in string theory
                %strong 244
                2–3 (1994) 77-202 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-1573(94)90070-1"}> doi:10.1016/0370-1573(94)90070-1
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                E. Alvarez,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Alvarez-Gaum%C3%A9"} Luis Alvarez-Gaumé
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yolanda+Lozano"} Yolanda Lozano
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to T-Duality in String Theory
                %strong 41
                (1995) 1-20 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9410237"}> arXiv:hep-th/9410237
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0920-5632(95)00429-D"} doi:10.1016/0920-5632(95)00429-D
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Rosenberg"}> Jonathan Rosenberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Topology,
                    = succeed "-algebras," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 111
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/cbms/111"} doi:10.1090/cbms/111
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:05632075&type=pdf&format=complete"} ZMATH
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (focus on
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
            %li#West12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+West"}> Peter West
                , section 17.1 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Strings+and+Branes"} Introduction to Strings and Branes
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139045926"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139045926
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathai+Varghese"}> Mathai Varghese
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em T-duality: A basic introduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Geometry
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Mathai12.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Jnanadeva Maharana,
                %em The Worldsheet Perspective of T-duality Symmetry in String Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1302.1719"}> arXiv:1302.1719
                )
            %li
              %p
                Mark Bugden,
                %em A Tour of T-duality: Geometric and Topological Aspects of T-dualities
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.03583"}> arXiv:1904.03583
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion at strong coupling (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"}> F-theory
            ”):
          %ul
            %li#Schwarz96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"}> John Schwarz
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em M Theory Extensions of T Duality
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Frontiers in Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9601077"} arXiv:hep-th/9601077
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/3245"} doi:10.1142/3245
            %li#Russo97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Russo"}> Jorge Russo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em T-duality in M-theory and supermembranes
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9701188"} arXiv:hep-th/9701188
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            formulation of T-duality in the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
          %p
            via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+spinor+superstring"} pure spinor superstring
          %ul
            %li
              Raphael Benichou,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giuseppe+Policastro"} Giuseppe Policastro
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jan+Troost"} Jan Troost
              = succeed "," do
                %em T-duality in Ramond-Ramond backgrounds
              %strong 661
              (2008) 192-195 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.1785"}> arXiv:0801.1785
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physletb.2008.01.059"} doi:10.1016/j.physletb.2008.01.059
          %p
            Dscussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
          %ul
            %li#Alfonsi19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Global Double Field Theory is Higher Kaluza-Klein Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.07089"} arXiv:1912.07089
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.202000010"} doi:10.1002/prop.202000010
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (relating
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
                  on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
                  to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"} double field theory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-folds"} T-folds
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+T-duality"} non-abelian T-duality
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+geometry"} type II geometry
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+geometry"} exceptional geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
                ,
                %em The puzzle of global Double Field Theory: open problems and the case for a Higher Kaluza-Klein perspective
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.04969"}> arXiv:2007.04969
                )
          %p
            Discussion of geometric T-duality in terms of some form of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            as an operation on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
          %ul
            %li#KahleValentino
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Kahle"}> Alexander Kahle
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Valentino"} Alessandro Valentino
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-Duality+and+Differential+K-Theory"} T-Duality and Differential K-Theory
          %p
            using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjusted+Weil+algebra"} adjusted
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-connections"} principal 2-connections
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hyungrok+Kim"}> Hyungrok Kim
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"} Christian Saemann
                %em Non-Geometric T-Duality as Higher Groupoid Bundles with Connections
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.01783"}> arXiv:2204.01783
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"}> Christian Saemann
                \:
                %em Non-Geometric T-duality from
              %p
                Higher Groupoid Bundles with Connections
                %em> , talk at
                ESI Workshop on Higher Structures* (2022) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.esi.ac.at/uploads/00c2f5b8-cbb4-413e-a07c-039c22a59b48.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hyungrok+Kim"}> Hyungrok Kim
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"} Christian Saemann
                = succeed "," do
                  %em T-duality as Correspondences of Categorified Principal Bundles with Adjusted Connections
                = succeed "," do
                  %em CORFU2022
                %strong 436
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16162"}> arXiv:2303.16162
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.22323/1.436.0336"} doi:10.22323/1.436.0336
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"}> Konrad Waldorf
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric T-duality: Buscher rules in general topology
                %strong 25
                (2024) 1285–1358 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.11799"}> arXiv:2207.11799
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00023-023-01295-0"} doi:10.1007/s00023-023-01295-0
          %p More physically oriented discussion of this is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Becker"}> Katrin Becker
              , Aaron Bergman,
              %em Geometric Aspects of D-branes and T-duality
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0908.2249"}> arXiv:0908.2249
              )
          %p
            Geometric T-duality is identified as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+Courant+algebroid"} standard Courant algebroid
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+complex+geometry"} generalized complex geometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gil+Cavalcanti"}> Gil Cavalcanti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Gualtieri"} Marco Gualtieri
              %em Generalized complex geometry and T-duality
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1106.1747"}> arXiv:1106.1747
              )
          %p
            Discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            description of T-duality in this context includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulf+Lindst%C3%B6m"}> Ulf Lindstöm
                ,
